# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in your own values. `.env` is gitignored —
# never commit real credentials, not even testnet ones.
#
# This file is *parsed*, not sourced: `KEY=VALUE` lines only, one optional layer
# of quotes stripped, nothing expanded and nothing executed. A real environment
# variable always wins over a value here, so `DOCTOR_NETWORK=local ./run.sh`
# works.
#
# Everything here is optional. With no `.env` at all, the doctor still runs
# every transport check — DNS, the base URL and its path prefix, the WebSocket
# bases, the clock, the venue's health. Credentials only add the last check.

# ─── credentials (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used for exactly one read-only call: `nexus orders`, to answer "can this
# client authenticate?" once the transport checks have established what a
# failure would mean. Nothing is ever placed, cancelled or changed.
#
# Mint a testnet pair with `nexus keys create` (see the CLI's README), or run
# `nexus setup` and let the CLI store them — this tool reads whichever it finds.
#
# NOTE the names. The CLI reads NEXUS_API_KEY / NEXUS_API_SECRET. The Nexus
# *SDKs* — and most other examples in this catalog — read
# NEXUS_EXCHANGE_API_KEY / NEXUS_EXCHANGE_API_SECRET. Copying a `.env` over from
# one of those examples leaves this one unauthenticated. The doctor has a check
# for exactly that, because the only other symptom is silence.
NEXUS_API_KEY=
NEXUS_API_SECRET=

# ─── where to look ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which network to diagnose. testnet (play funds, the default), mainnet, or
# local.
#
# Unlike `cli/quote-ladder`, mainnet is *allowed* here, and that is deliberate:
# this tool is read-only, and "why can I not reach mainnet?" is a question it
# exists to answer. As of 2026-09-09 the answer is that `api.nexus.xyz` resolves
# to no address at all, and saying so plainly beats a timeout.
DOCTOR_NETWORK=testnet

# The API base URL, overriding the per-network default. The catalog-wide
# override (CONTRIBUTING §4), honoured here for the same reason: a reader whose
# default host is unreachable needs a way to point the diagnostic at the thing
# being diagnosed.
#
# The prefix is part of the base and is load-bearing. On testnet the API is
# mounted under `/indexer`, and the bare host 404s:
#
#   https://api.testnet.nexus.xyz/indexer/markets/summary   -> 200
#   https://api.testnet.nexus.xyz/markets/summary           -> 404
#
# NEXUS_EXCHANGE_API_URL=https://api.testnet.nexus.xyz/indexer

# The WebSocket base. Defaults to the REST base with the scheme swapped, which
# is the point: deriving it from the bare origin instead drops the `/indexer`
# prefix, and the connect fails with a 404 while REST keeps working. Set this
# only if your deployment really does serve WS from somewhere else.
# NEXUS_EXCHANGE_WS_URL=wss://api.testnet.nexus.xyz/indexer

# ─── thresholds ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per-request timeout, and a shorter one for the WebSocket upgrade probe. A
# successful upgrade leaves the connection open with nothing to close it, so a
# 101 costs the whole WS timeout — keep it small.
DOCTOR_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15
DOCTOR_WS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=8

# How far this host's clock may sit from the venue's before it is a warning.
# Signed requests carry a timestamp the venue checks against a narrow window,
# and drift past it is rejected as a replay — with the same opaque 401 a wrong
# key gets.
DOCTOR_CLOCK_SKEW_WARN=5

# How far the indexer may lag the engine before it is a warning. Reads still
# answer while it lags; they are just that stale.
DOCTOR_LAG_WARN_SECONDS=60

# Set to 1 to skip the credential check entirely, even with credentials
# configured — for running this in CI, or in a shell you would rather not have
# it authenticate from.
DOCTOR_SKIP_CREDENTIALS=0
